WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.669
Idea. Every business turns inputs into output. Workers give their time, which is called labour, and the business makes goods or services from it.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:22.516
Imagine two students washing cars at a school fair. Ali washes twelve cars in three hours. Sara washes twelve cars in four hours. They wash the same number, but who works faster?

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:34.821
Divide the cars by the hours. Ali washes four cars per hour. Sara washes three cars per hour. Ali is more productive: he gets more output from each hour of work.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:46.039
Method. Labour productivity equals total output divided by the labour input. The labour input is either the hours worked or the number of workers.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:52.893
So the method is: find the total output, find the labour input, then divide and state the unit.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:01:06.003
The unit matters. Per hour means divide by hours. Per worker means divide by workers. For example, five hundred chairs made by twenty-five workers is twenty chairs per worker.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:13.991
Why does this matter? Higher productivity means more output from the same labour, so the wage cost of each unit falls.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:26.661
Worked example. Here is a real question from Paper two in November twenty twenty-five. Calculate the factory's productivity of smartphones per hour. It is worth two marks.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:37.660
The factory makes one thousand smartphones in twenty hours. One thousand divided by twenty is fifty. So productivity is fifty smartphones per hour.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:49.231
Common mistake. A common mistake is dividing upside down: hours divided by output. That gives a tiny number, which tells you something has gone wrong.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:57.266
Other students multiply instead of dividing, or give the number with no unit. Divide, then write the full unit.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:10.440
Your turn. Now you try. Six bakers make seven hundred and twenty loaves in an eight-hour shift. Calculate the labour productivity in loaves per worker. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:29.073
Answer. The question says per worker, so divide by the six bakers. Seven hundred and twenty divided by six is one hundred and twenty loaves per worker. Check: one hundred and twenty times six is seven hundred and twenty. The eight-hour shift is not needed.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:38.747
Remember the method: find the output, find the labour input, then divide and state the unit. Now try the practice questions for this type.
